    "id": "141d90b9-cbf8-4ebc-9e07-61462aff1d48",
    "category": "Entertainment",
    "title": "Why One Chorus Can Follow You Home",
    "excerpt": "A catchy chorus can replay in your head long after the music stops. The science of earworms explains why the brain keeps pressing repeat.",
    "body": "You leave the shop, the bus or a film, and suddenly the same four bars are playing in your mind. No headphones. No singer. Just a chorus that refuses to clock out. Researchers call this involuntary musical imagery; most people simply call it an earworm.\n\nStudies suggest that these loops are a normal part of how the brain remembers music. Familiar tunes, repeated listening and a strong melodic hook can all help a fragment return. One large survey found that many earworms have a quick tempo and a melody that is easy to sing but distinctive enough to stand out.\n\nThat does not mean a song has secretly taken control of your brain. The experience is common and usually harmless. It may show up when your mind has spare room, like during a walk, a shower or a routine chore.\n\nIf the chorus is keeping you awake, research suggests that trying to force it away may make it more noticeable. You could listen to the whole song once, switch to another absorbing activity or let the melody pass without treating it like an emergency. Then try not to hum this paragraph.\n\nWhy do some songs stick while others disappear? A hook often gives the mind a small, repeatable shape: a rise, a pause, then a note that lands somewhere satisfying. If the tune is familiar, your memory has a ready-made path to follow. A fragment can be enough to restart that path, even when you cannot remember choosing to think about it.\n\nThere is a useful experiment hiding in the next earworm. Notice what came just before it: a lyric on a shop speaker, a mood, a place, or a task that left your mind wandering. You may find a connection, or you may find that the tune arrived without a clear trigger. Either way, the loop is a small example of memory working by association, not a sign that anything is wrong. Try listening to the full track once and then switching to a different activity. The goal is not to win a fight with a melody; it is to give your attention something else to follow.",
    "source_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32583211/\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34105416/",
    "published_at": "2026-10-28",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:00:00Z",
